# dev-snapshot default ruleset
#
# Tab-separated: kind <TAB> name <TAB> guard <TAB> why
#
#   kind   dir   a directory, matched by exact name, excluded with everything under it
#          file  a filename glob, handed to tar as a pattern
#
#   guard  -            no guard; the name alone is proof enough
#          sibling:F    exclude only if F exists NEXT TO the candidate directory
#          has:F        exclude only if F exists INSIDE the candidate directory
#          several guards joined by | are an OR
#
# Guards exist because a directory name is a weak claim. `vendor` is a Composer
# cache next to a composer.json and hand-written source everywhere else, and a
# `vendor/` can hold cloned git repositories with uncommitted work in them. A
# rule that went on the name alone would throw those away and report a
# successful backup.
#
# `file` rules take no guard: they become tar patterns rather than enumerated
# paths, so there is no candidate to test. Keep them to things that are junk
# under any circumstances.
#
# This is a starting set, not a claim about your tree. Add your own in
# ~/.config/dev-snapshot/rules -- same format, applied after these.

# --- dependency trees, restored by a package manager ------------------------
dir	node_modules	-	npm/yarn/pnpm install
dir	bower_components	-	bower install
dir	vendor	sibling:composer.json	composer install
dir	Pods	sibling:Podfile	pod install
dir	.pub-cache	-	dart/flutter package cache

# --- virtualenvs ------------------------------------------------------------
# Guarded on pyvenv.cfg, the file that actually makes a directory a virtualenv.
# `env` and `venv` are otherwise ordinary words and people name source dirs that.
dir	.venv	has:pyvenv.cfg	python virtualenv
dir	venv	has:pyvenv.cfg	python virtualenv
dir	env	has:pyvenv.cfg	python virtualenv
dir	virtualenv	has:pyvenv.cfg	python virtualenv

# --- interpreter and tool caches --------------------------------------------
dir	__pycache__	-	python bytecode cache
dir	.pytest_cache	-	pytest cache
dir	.mypy_cache	-	mypy cache
dir	.ruff_cache	-	ruff cache
dir	.tox	-	tox environments
dir	.nox	-	nox environments
dir	.eggs	-	setuptools build eggs
dir	.gradle	-	gradle project cache
dir	.sass-cache	-	sass cache
dir	.parcel-cache	-	parcel cache
dir	.turbo	-	turborepo cache
dir	.cache	-	generic tool cache

# --- framework build output, regenerated by a build ------------------------
dir	.next	-	next.js build output
dir	.nuxt	-	nuxt build output
dir	.svelte-kit	-	sveltekit build output
dir	.angular	-	angular cli cache
dir	.dart_tool	-	dart build output
dir	target	sibling:Cargo.toml|sibling:pom.xml	cargo/maven build output

# --- downloaded provider and plugin binaries --------------------------------
# .terraform holds provider binaries that are hundreds of megabytes each and
# come back with `terraform init`. The lockfile beside it is what pins them,
# and that file is source, so it stays.
dir	.terraform	-	terraform providers and modules

# --- coverage and test artifacts --------------------------------------------
dir	.nyc_output	-	nyc coverage intermediates
dir	htmlcov	-	coverage html report

# --- files that are junk under any circumstances ----------------------------
file	.DS_Store	-	macos finder metadata
file	._*	-	macos resource fork
file	Thumbs.db	-	windows explorer thumbnails
file	*.pyc	-	python bytecode
file	*.pyo	-	python bytecode
file	*.swp	-	vim swap file
file	*~	-	editor backup file

# --- deliberately NOT here, and each for a reason ---------------------------
#
# .git          The history is most of what a backup is for, and on a machine
#               where repositories have no remote it is the ONLY copy.
# dist, build   Both are real, committed source often enough to matter, and on
#               the tree this was written for they came to 86 MB outside the
#               dependency trees already excluded above. Not worth the risk.
# .env          Configuration, frequently irreplaceable. This is why the
#               archive is encrypted rather than why it should be thinner.
# *.log         Sometimes the only record of what a service did.
